WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.750
Who do you think of when you hear the words
“father of the atomic bomb”?

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:28.130
We’re guessing it’s Robert Oppenheimer,
the leader of the Manhattan Project who famously

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:35.160
responded to the first A-Bomb test by declaring
“now I am become Death, destroyer of worlds.”

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.470
But what if we told you there was another
contender?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:43.301
A scientist who came this close to making
the big breakthrough in 1934… a breakthrough

00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:47.960
that could have resulted in Nazi Germany developing
the world’s first nuclear weapon.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.780
The scientist who almost created this terrifying
alternative reality was Enrico Fermi, the

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.320
godfather of the atom bomb.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:03.079
A leading light in both experimental and theoretical
physics, Fermi was an almost once-in-a-generation

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:04.079
genius.

00:01:04.079 --> 00:01:08.860
It was Fermi who built the first operational
nuclear reactor in 1942, Fermi who created

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:14.440
our understanding of the subatomic particles
known today as fermions, and Fermi who helped

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.500
Oppenheimer father the Bomb.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.600
From fascist Italy to WWII America, this is
the life of Enrico Fermi, the greatest Italian

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.970
scientist this side of Galileo.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:31.140
The Quiet Italian

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.160
One of themes we’ve touched on, time and
again is that - despite what fiction tells

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:40.880
you - you usually can’t tell which child
is gonna grow up to change the world.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.640
Otto von Bismarck, for example, was lazy as
a kid.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.130
Mathematician Emmy Noether was going to be
a language teacher.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.700
Johannes Kepler was obsessed with joining
the priesthood.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.580
But there are times when even real life follows
the rules of fiction.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.330
Times when you really can just look at a child
and think “that kid’s gonna be world-famous

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.330
one day.”

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.730
Enrico Fermi was one of those kids.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.860
Born in Rome on September 29, 1901, Fermi
came from an average family.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.430
His father was a government railway inspector,
while his mother Ida was a teacher.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.310
But Ida was above average in one very particular
way.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:19.440
She believed acquiring knowledge was almost
a sacred duty.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.270
You might say this was the closest thing to
a religion in the Fermi household.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:27.740
Unusually for the time, the family weren’t
Catholic, and sent young Fermi to one of the

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.640
few secular schools in Rome.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.820
There, aged only six, Enrico Fermi began to
show signs of the genius he would become.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.590
Basically, from the moment he started school,
Fermi was consistently at the top of his class.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:45.510
Ida had instilled in him a deep love for math,
science, engineering, and everything else

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.510
brainy.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:49.260
When not studying, the boy spent his time
building electric motors with his older brother

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.260
Giulio.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.430
So, yeah, if you’d been introduced to child
Fermi circa 1910, you’d have probably thought

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.660
to yourself “that kid’s gonna be big.”

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.280
But you might not have guessed he’d be big
in the world of physics.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.880
That’s because it’d take a tragedy to
set Fermi on that particular path.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.440
In January, 1915, Fermi’s brother Giulio
went into hospital for routine surgery on

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:10.970
a throat abscess.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:15.540
Something went wrong, and he died on the operating
table, bleeding out aged only 15.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:20.930
For the teenage Fermi, this was like God had
just cruelly hurled a lightning bolt right

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.560
into the middle of his life.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.120
As a boy, Fermi had always been shy and introverted.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.960
But in the wake of Giulio’s death, he became
almost catatonically withdrawn.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.850
Frightened, Ida tried to convince her son
to lose himself in his studies.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:37.440
She found a fifty year old book on physics,
and gave it to the boy to read.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.960
What Fermi read there blew his young mind.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.550
There was something about the world described
in that book that just made sense to Fermi,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.990
that made him want to learn more.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:51.300
From that point on, the boy wouldn’t be
a magpie, leaping from one STEM subject to

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.300
the next.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:57.410
His mind would instead be a high-powered laser,
focused solely on physics.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:02.209
In 1918, three years after Giulio died, Fermi
applied to the prestigious Scuola Normale

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:03.959
Superiore in Pisa.

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:07.010
On November 14 that year he sat the entrance
exam.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.600
The task was to write a competitive essay.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.400
Fermi chose the topic Characteristics of Sound.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.340
When the examiner read it, he nearly fell
off his chair.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.840
While anyone sitting this exam was naturally
intelligent, Fermi was at a whole other level.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.310
He’d turned in a paper that would’ve been
impressive for a doctoral thesis.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.120
Fermi was instantly fast-tracked onto the
school’s doctoral program, with his advisor

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.370
the director of the physics laboratory.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.440
But the director soon realized this kid was
such a natural that it was impossible to teach

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.560
him stuff.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.650
In just three years of self-study, Fermi had
become so good at physics that he could run

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.910
rings around a department chair.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.530
Everybody at the school could agree on one
thing.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:48.530
They couldn’t wait to see what he did next.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.030
Breaking Italy

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:57.120
At the same time Fermi was rising through
the physics profession like an out of control

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.570
weather balloon, another Italian also found
himself being catapulted toward stardom.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:08.180
On March 23, 1919 - mere months after Fermi
sat his exam - Benito Mussolini founded the

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.320
Fascist Party.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.460
Although neither Fermi nor Mussolini could’ve
known it at the time, their lives were destined

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.340
to cross in a way that would change history.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.770
Within a year of the Fascist Party’s appearance,
chapters had sprung up across the nation,

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.410
and whole regions descended into violence.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:27.850
Mussolini was a world class rabble rouser,
a guy who could whip up a mob in the time

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.370
it takes to boil an egg.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.710
As his influence spread, blackshirt militias
began attacking leftists, burning down trade

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:38.340
union offices, and physically stopping leftwing
officials from taking their posts.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:45.310
Come July, 1922, the whole of Italy knew Mussolini’s
name, and either loved or loathed him.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.430
With possibly one exception.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:52.530
All his life, Enrico Fermi would be staunchly
apolitical, never showing the slightest hint

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.310
of interest in politics.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:59.560
As Italy fell into chaos that summer, he was
likely thinking of one thing only: defending

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.670
his thesis.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:05.550
By now, Fermi had already published his first
couple of papers, and his planet-sized brain

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.520
was infamous across Pisa.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.720
When he actually came to defend his thesis
before 11 examiners, it’s said only two

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:15.139
of them were capable of grasping what he was
talking about.

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:19.710
Fermi graduated with honors, almost immediately
receiving a government grant to continue his

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:20.710
studies.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.910
Not that that government would be around much
longer.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:28.520
That same summer, Mussolini’s blackshirts
smashed a general strike called by Italian

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.520
leftists.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.960
On 24 October, before a gathering of 40,000
fascists, Il Duce declared that the government

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.190
was incapable of governing, and that his party
would physically remove it from power.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:44.050
Mussolini’s March on Rome was the moment
democracy died in Italy.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.090
Faced with an insurrection, the government
collapsed.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:51.160
Preferring fascism to anarchy, King Victor
Emmanuel III invited Mussolini to form a new

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:52.210
government.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:56.910
On Halloween that year - a fitting date if
there ever was one - Italy’s balder, fatter

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:01.620
version of Hitler became Prime Minister, the
first fascist leader in history.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.210
But while life was about to take a bad turn
for Italy in general, it was taking a series

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.600
of extremely good turns for Enrico Fermi.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.680
Between 1922 and 1926, Fermi won a series
of scholarships, going abroad to teach in

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.240
Germany and the Netherlands.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.760
By the time he settled back in Italy in 1926,
he was ready to change the world.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:25.620
Offered the chair of theoretical physics at
the University of Rome, aged just 25, Fermi

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.710
made breakthrough after breakthrough.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:32.479
Perhaps his most lasting was the development
of what are known as Fermi-Dirac statistics

00:07:32.479 --> 00:07:36.389
- basically a way of understanding a group
of subatomic particles that would otherwise

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:37.860
make us go “whaaaaaaaaa-?”

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.540
every time we thought about them.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:45.010
In fact, Fermi was so prolific, and his intuition
so often accurate, that his colleagues started

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.270
calling him “the Pope” of physics.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.880
But it wasn’t just Fermi’s colleagues
who were paying attention to his extraordinary

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:51.880
mind.

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:57.340
In 1929, Benito Mussolini - now absolute dictator
of Italy - personally appointed Fermi to the

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:58.760
Accademia dei Lincei.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:03.310
The apolitical physicist was given a huge
salary, a uniform to wear, and the title of

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:04.310
“excellency.”

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:09.700
All he had to do in return was bring glory
upon Mussolini’s Italy by making more breakthroughs.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.910
Scarily, that’s exactly what happened.

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:18.810
In his new post, Fermi would soon come terrifyingly
close to giving Il Duce the key to the atom

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:19.810
bomb.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:22.770
Fathering the Bomb

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:28.200
The next few years were crazy-busy for Fermi.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.810
In 1928, he married Laura Capon, daughter
of a respected Jewish family, with whom he’d

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:33.810
have two children.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:37.930
In 1930, he made his first visit to America,
where he lectured on quantum theory.

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:42.729
Shortly after, Fermi seems to have decided
the quantum field was becoming exhausted.

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:45.330
He switched his focus to nuclear physics.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:48.040
It was this switch that would lead to his
big breakthrough.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:51.079
In 1934, Fermi and his team were researching
the atom.

00:08:51.079 --> 00:08:55.769
They’d already discovered that pretty much
every element is capable of nuclear transformation,

00:08:55.769 --> 00:08:57.889
and were now focusing on uranium.

00:08:57.889 --> 00:09:01.959
It was while working with the element that
Fermi discovered the first step toward nuclear

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:02.959
fission.

00:09:02.959 --> 00:09:06.449
Since we’re a history channel rather than
a science channel, we’re not qualified to

00:09:06.449 --> 00:09:08.160
go deep into this.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.019
But the basic gist is that Fermi made a breakthrough,
from which any garden-variety genius could’ve

00:09:14.019 --> 00:09:18.360
deduced other breakthroughs could be made,
eventually leading to everything from nuclear

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.709
reactors to A-Bombs.

00:09:20.709 --> 00:09:25.959
As the 2016 book The Pope of Physics notes,
had Fermi’s brain been firing on all cylinders

00:09:25.959 --> 00:09:31.249
at that time, it’s likely nuclear fission
would’ve been discovered in Italy in 1935.

00:09:31.249 --> 00:09:33.430
One possible consequence?

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:37.160
Nazi Germany using that research to acquire
nuclear weapons.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:41.540
It’s a scary thought that out there, just
a few degrees away, sits a parallel universe

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:46.319
where the London Blitz was not a sustained
campaign, but a single bomb, falling from

00:09:46.319 --> 00:09:51.809
the sky, that turned the British capital into
rubble in a flash of light.

00:09:51.809 --> 00:09:54.559
Luckily, we don’t live in that universe.

00:09:54.559 --> 00:09:57.449
In our universe, Fermi’s brain - for some
reason - wasn’t quite operating at full

00:09:57.449 --> 00:09:58.449
capacity.

00:09:58.449 --> 00:10:03.110
He merely thought he’d created a new element;
one the press argued should be named after

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.110
Mussolini.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.319
By the time everyone figured out what was
actually going on, any headstart Nazi Germany

00:10:08.319 --> 00:10:10.939
could’ve got on the Bomb was gone.

00:10:10.939 --> 00:10:14.269
In terms of near misses, they don’t get
much nearer than that.

00:10:14.269 --> 00:10:18.360
Still, Fermi’s work was important, important
enough for him to be awarded the Nobel Prize

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.540
for Physics in 1938.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:25.199
And that’s today’s second stroke of good
luck, because 1938 was when everything went

00:10:25.199 --> 00:10:26.199
to hell.

00:10:26.199 --> 00:10:30.149
That summer, Mussolini suddenly brought in
a raft of anti-Jewish laws.

00:10:30.149 --> 00:10:35.959
So extreme were these laws, so nakedly modeled
on Hitler’s, that they penetrated even Fermi’s

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:37.179
physics-addled mind.

00:10:37.179 --> 00:10:40.480
Although he was staunchly apolitical, Fermi
couldn’t ignore the fact that these new

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.160
laws targeted his wife; and probably their
children.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.620
Unfortunately, the new laws also made it impossible
for Jews to leave Italy without a very good

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:49.620
reason.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.360
A reason like, oh, I dunno… winning the
Nobel Prize?

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:57.179
That December, Fermi traveled to Sweden to
collect his Nobel Prize for Physics.

00:10:57.179 --> 00:11:01.639
He took his family with him, the no-travel
laws for Jews briefly waived on the understanding

00:11:01.639 --> 00:11:03.929
that the award would bring glory on Italy.

00:11:03.929 --> 00:11:09.499
In Stockholm, Fermi accepted his award, then
used his prize money to get himself, his wife,

00:11:09.499 --> 00:11:12.329
and his children on a boat to the USA.

00:11:12.329 --> 00:11:16.500
And that’s how Italy’s greatest modern
scientist came to leave his country behind

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:17.500
for good.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:21.860
It’s curious to think that, had Mussolini
not suddenly passed those anti-Jewish laws,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.889
Fermi would’ve likely stayed in Italy for
the duration of the war.

00:11:24.889 --> 00:11:28.100
All the work he’d do under Oppenheimer would
be lost.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:32.420
Lost, too, would be the world’s first nuclear
reactor, soon to be built under Fermi’s

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:33.420
watch in Chicago.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:38.959
And Fermi was just one of thousands of scientists
who fled the continent in those days.

00:11:38.959 --> 00:11:44.089
Had Europe’s fascists not become so obsessed
with national purity, their nations would

00:11:44.089 --> 00:11:46.610
ironically have lasted so much longer.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:50.059
On January 2, 1939, the Fermis arrived in
New York City.

00:11:50.059 --> 00:12:16.569
For the rest of their lives, the Fermis would
call America “home”.

00:12:16.569 --> 00:13:21.910
“A labor of considerable scientific interest”

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:26.749
Barely had Fermi set foot inside Columbia
than he was already gifting his new government

00:13:26.749 --> 00:13:27.790
breakthroughs.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:32.629
That same spring, 1939, the Italian and his
team showed that emitting neutrons into uranium

00:13:32.629 --> 00:13:36.490
that was in the process of fissioning could
trigger a chain reaction.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.899
In other words: KA-BOOM.

00:13:38.899 --> 00:13:42.579
By March, the university was already writing
to the Navy, suggesting that this could be

00:13:42.579 --> 00:13:44.070
used as a weapon.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.829
Nor was Fermi himself blind to this possibility.

00:13:46.829 --> 00:13:50.860
One day in a Manhattan highrise, he glanced
out a window at the bustling city and murmured

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:54.240
“a little bomb like that and it would all
disappear.”

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:59.629
But Fermi was no Oppenheimer, tortured inside
by the destruction his research could wreak.

00:13:59.629 --> 00:14:04.689
He actually went to Washington, DC personally
to try and drum up funding for nuclear weapons

00:14:04.689 --> 00:14:05.689
research.

00:14:05.689 --> 00:14:10.759
But his presentation was so singularly uncharismatic
that he walked away with only the smallest

00:14:10.759 --> 00:14:11.759
of grants.

00:14:11.759 --> 00:14:16.009
It wasn’t until Albert Einstein personally
wrote to Franklin D Roosevelt about the potential

00:14:16.009 --> 00:14:20.499
for superweapons that the American government
began to really show interest.

00:14:20.499 --> 00:14:22.800
Still, it was a slow process.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:27.929
Only on December 6, 1941 was a project to
study these theories in Chicago signed off

00:14:27.929 --> 00:14:28.929
on.

00:14:28.929 --> 00:14:32.879
But if things had been moving slowly for Fermi’s
research up until that point, they were about

00:14:32.879 --> 00:14:35.269
to gain a sense of extreme urgency.

00:14:35.269 --> 00:14:37.230
So, you remember history class.

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.389
You know what happened on December 7, 1941.

00:14:40.389 --> 00:14:45.529
The Japanese bombing of Pearl Harbor dragged
America kicking and screaming into the war.

00:14:45.529 --> 00:14:50.740
It also had the side effect that every Italian
in the country was immediately classed an

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:54.249
“enemy alien,” and subjected to severe
travel restrictions.

00:14:54.249 --> 00:14:57.970
It took nearly six months for Fermi’s restrictions
to be waived for his research.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:02.170
He finally arrived in Chicago in summer, 1942.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:06.119
But once he was there, Fermi almost immediately
proved his worth.

00:15:06.119 --> 00:15:11.370
We already spoke one terrifying parallel universe
earlier, where the Axis got the Bomb.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.579
Now we’re going to speak about a much weirder
one.

00:15:13.579 --> 00:15:19.139
The universe where this video isn’t titled
“Enrico Fermi: Godfather of the Atomic Bomb”,

00:15:19.139 --> 00:15:23.069
but “Enrico Fermi: the Mad Scientist who
Destroyed Chicago.”

00:15:23.069 --> 00:15:27.310
Fermi and his team assembled a nuclear reactor
on the squash court of the University of Chicago

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.439
toward the end of 1942.

00:15:29.439 --> 00:15:31.709
On December 2, they sent it critical.

00:15:31.709 --> 00:15:34.829
As the New York Times later wrote of the potentially-dangerous
experiment:

00:15:34.829 --> 00:15:39.240
“The only safeguards against an uncontrolled
atomic chain reaction that would have irradiated

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:44.379
a significant part of Chicago was a scientist
wielding an ax to cut the rope that held an

00:15:44.379 --> 00:15:49.929
emergency control rod suspended from the balcony,
and a few other brave volunteers, whose job

00:15:49.929 --> 00:15:55.029
was to douse the runaway reactor with buckets
of neutron-absorbing cadmium sulfate.”

00:15:55.029 --> 00:15:59.269
Fermi actually joked that, if the reactor
went out of control, the assembled team should

00:15:59.269 --> 00:16:02.759
run and hide behind a big hill many miles
away.

00:16:02.759 --> 00:16:07.759
But, of course, Chicago Pile-1 didn’t become
Chernobyl before Chernobyl.

00:16:07.759 --> 00:16:13.059
It worked, creating a self-sustaining atomic
chain reaction - the first working nuclear

00:16:13.059 --> 00:16:14.119
reactor in history.

00:16:14.119 --> 00:16:19.129
Shortly after, a code was broadcast to Washington,
DC: “The Italian navigator has just landed

00:16:19.129 --> 00:16:20.669
in the new world”.

00:16:20.669 --> 00:16:23.839
It was the dawn of the atomic age.

00:16:23.839 --> 00:16:27.379
It was also the dawn of a new age in Fermi’s
work.

00:16:27.379 --> 00:16:31.680
In 1944, the Italian physicist became a naturalized
US citizen.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:36.079
Shortly after, he was sent down to Los Alamos
to work on the Manhattan Project under Robert

00:16:36.079 --> 00:16:37.079
Oppenheimer.

00:16:37.079 --> 00:16:41.769
Fermi was actually there on July 16, 1945,
when the first Plutonium bomb was tested,

00:16:41.769 --> 00:16:45.519
at the infamous Trinity test in Alamogordo,
New Mexico.

00:16:45.519 --> 00:16:49.160
This was the moment when Oppenheimer, seeing
the power he had unleashed, thought the phrase

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:52.019
“I am become death, destroyer of worlds.”

00:16:52.019 --> 00:16:56.379
But - as we’ve said before - Fermi was no
Oppenhemier.

00:16:56.379 --> 00:17:00.860
Faced with the reality of a weapon capable
of wiping out entire cities in the blink of

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:06.549
an eye, Fermi only noted that the Trinity
test was “a labor of considerable scientific

00:17:06.549 --> 00:17:07.549
interest.”

00:17:07.549 --> 00:17:13.010
How I Learned to Stop Worrying...

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:16.630
In the last months of the Manhattan Project,
Fermi’s dedication to building the Bomb

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:17.630
didn’t waver.

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:21.949
Although in April, he noted that their labors
would one day be able to be reproduced by

00:17:21.949 --> 00:17:26.260
“smaller nations or even groups,” he still
thought it a worthwhile endeavour.

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:29.960
That June, he joined the Scientific Panel
of the Interim Committee, a group tasked - in

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:33.980
part - with advising on the use of atomic
weapons against Japan.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:37.510
Just like his colleagues, Fermi advocated
for an atomic strike.

00:17:37.510 --> 00:17:41.740
Although some members of the panel did object
on ethical grounds, preferring instead a public

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:46.270
test that would give Japan warning, President
Harry Truman signed off on an attack.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:52.320
Just two months later, on August 6, 1945,
a bomb distilled from the work of Fermi, Oppenheimer,

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:58.200
and the other geniuses at Los Alamos was loaded
onto the Enola Gay and flown off into the

00:17:58.200 --> 00:17:59.460
night sky.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:03.990
Six hours later, the Enola Gay’s bomb bay
doors opened above the commercial and military

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:05.980
city of Hiroshima.

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:08.650
Little Boy dropped for 44 seconds.

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.730
At the end of that time, the world changed
forever.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:19.100
The first atomic bomb ever used in warfare
detonated at precisely 08:16:02 am some 609

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:20.650
meters above Hiroshima.

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:28.950
Within 0.1 of a second, the air over the city
center had reached 277,760 C. A woman sat

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:33.910
nearly a kilometer away on the banks of the
city’s river was instantly vaporized.

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:38.720
As the fireball expanded, granite stones melted,
the internal organs of humans and animals

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:42.790
vaporized, and exposed skin was burned off.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:48.390
By the time a single second had passed, a
blast wave traveling at 1,500 km/h had torn

00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:54.190
the clothes off nearly everyone in the city,
destroyed 60,000 buildings; and an intense

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:58.200
firestorm had started that would rage for
hours and hours.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:03.620
In that instant, 80,000 people died, including
up to 20,000 Korean slave laborers and over

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:05.820
a dozen Allied POWs.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:10.190
Many tens of thousands more would die of radiation
poisoning over the coming weeks.

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:13.530
It was carnage on a previously unimaginable
scale.

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:15.160
And it wasn’t over.

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:19.050
Three days later, on August 9, another plane
carrying another bomb flew over Japan.

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:23.610
Originally, it was meant to drop its payload
on the ancient city of Kokura, but thick cloud

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:26.150
cover meant that Nagasaki became its target.

00:19:26.150 --> 00:19:31.180
At 11:02am, Fat Man exploded above the port
city, turning over 40,000 people into ash.

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:36.720
It was the second, and - to date - last time
atomic weapons were used in warfare.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.450
Six days later, Japan surrendered unconditionally.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:44.250
Today, it remains up for debate whether the
real motivation for Japanese surrender was

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:50.160
the two atomic bombs, or the USSR’s declaration
of war the same day as Nagasaki.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:55.140
Whatever the truth, there’s no denying that
the weapons Fermi helped build had just shocked

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:56.160
the world.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.130
By now, though, the war was already over in
Europe.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.850
In Italy, partisans had captured Mussolini
back in April, stringing up his corpse in

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:02.940
a Milan square.

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:07.290
Hitler, too, was dead - killed by a self-inflicted
gunshot in his Fhurerbunker.

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:11.560
The fascists who’d chased Fermi and his
family out of Europe were gone.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:15.180
The physicist could now go back to his home
country whenever he chose.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.210
But what remained in Italy for him?

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:19.520
His wife’s family were dead, killed in the
Holocaust.

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:21.760
Rome lay in ruins.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:27.740
Instead, in fall of 1945, Fermi accepted a
teaching position at the University of Chicago.

00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:36.990
He would live in the city for the rest of
his life.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:37.990
The Last Paradox

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:41.760
The last years of Enrico Fermi’s life were
marked by a return to the magpie-like fascination

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:43.640
that characterized his earlier years.

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:47.880
At Chicago, he moved on from nuclear physics
and began researching elementary particles,

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.130
before becoming obsessed with cosmic rays.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:54.510
Perhaps his most-famous contribution to science
in this time, though, came not in the form

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:57.300
of a discovery, but of a question.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:58.580
“Where is everybody?”

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:03.180
In 1950, Fermi was lunching with some colleagues
when they began to discuss alien civilizations.

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:08.790
This was three years after the supposed Roswell
Crash, a time when UFOs were all the rage.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:12.650
One by one, Fermi’s friends asserted that
there were undoubtedly aliens out there in

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:13.650
the galaxy.

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:15.820
It was at this point that Fermi asked his
question.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.890
“Where is everybody?” may sound like a
flip remark, but Fermi had a serious point.

00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:25.390
He’d realized that - given the immense age
of the galaxy - any civilization with even

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:29.450
the most rudimentary rocket technology should’ve
colonized it by now.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:33.980
Assuming even a hyper-conservative estimate
for how often an intelligent civilization

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:39.380
arises, the sheer time scales involved meant
there should be abundant evidence of aliens

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:40.380
by now.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:41.660
So, again.

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:42.660
Where is everyone?

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:47.520
Today, that simple question is known as the
Fermi Paradox, and the list of explanations

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:53.180
as to why there are no signs of alien life
could fill a dozen videos all by themselves.

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:57.290
Seriously, if you want to head down an infinite
click-hole, just type “the great filter”

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.980
or “dark forest theory” into Google.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:05.230
But while Fermi may have meddled in many disciplines
in his later years, he could never quite escape

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:09.940
the one thread running throughout his working
life: nuclear physics.

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:14.550
In 1949, Fermi joined the General Advisory
Committee to the Atomic Energy Commission.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:18.460
That year, the USSR had tested its first atomic
weapon, and the Committee had gathered to

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:24.620
decide on the practical and moral implications
of the USA developing thermonuclear devices.

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:28.360
Unlike the atomic bombing of Japan, Fermi
was steadfastly against the idea.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:33.300
A hydrogen bomb, he wrote in an dissension
from the majority opinion, “becomes a weapon

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:36.380
which in practical effect is almost one of
genocide….

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:41.050
It is necessarily an evil thing considered
in any light.”

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:45.570
After a lifetime with no political opinions
at all, it seemed Enrico Fermi was finally

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:50.400
waking up to a world where governments might
use his discoveries for evil.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.210
But this new mindset only went so far.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:58.140
When Truman signed off on the H-Bomb, Fermi
dutifully went back to Los Alamos to work

00:22:58.140 --> 00:22:59.140
on it.

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:01.760
It’s said he hoped to prove such a weapon
was impossible.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:04.530
If that’s the case, he was doomed to disappointment.

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:09.260
The world’s first hydrogen bomb was tested
in the Marshall Islands on November 1, 1952.

00:23:09.260 --> 00:23:14.190
The explosion was so vast, so world-changing
in its implications, that it graced the cover

00:23:14.190 --> 00:23:15.190
of Time.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:19.800
Not long after, Fermi came up with his own,
pessimistic answer for the Fermi Paradox.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:25.280
There were no alien civilizations because
they’d all wiped themselves out with thermonuclear

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:26.280
weapons.

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.460
Still, Fermi being Fermi, he carried on working.

00:23:28.460 --> 00:23:31.750
There were doubtless more discoveries to be
made.

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:36.450
In the summer of 1954, he even went back to
Italy, hiking in the mountains and enjoying

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:38.169
the air of the old country.

00:23:38.169 --> 00:23:40.180
At least, that was the plan.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:43.810
The reality was Fermi found himself feeling
constantly run down.

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:47.770
When he returned to Chicago at the end of
his vacation, he went straight to the doctor.

00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:52.240
Since you can already see we’re nearing
the end of the video, you can probably guess

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:53.620
what came next.

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:56.220
The doctors told Fermi he had stomach cancer.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:24:00.360
Although they tried to cure it, the disease
was already too far advanced.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:02.320
Enrico Fermi died on November 28, 1954.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:04.940
He was 53.

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:10.240
Today, Fermi’s name lives on - most notably
in the Paradox named after him, and the element

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:13.100
fermium, discovered in 1953.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:17.140
While it’s doubtful the average Joe has
heard of him, he remains highly-respected,

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:20.040
a genius who still gets his due.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:24.640
Yet its interesting to recall just how unlikely
this great reputation was.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:28.800
If things had been just a shade different,
Fermi could’ve gone down in history as the

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.440
man who accidentally gave the Axis the Bomb.

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:36.080
Or he could’ve become trapped in Italy in
1938, and perished alongside his wife in the

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.080
Holocaust.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:41.880
He may even have made it to America, only
to send Chicago Pile-1 out of control and

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:44.090
cause the world’s first nuclear meltdown.

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:49.430
Thankfully, this universe is one where disaster
never struck; where Fermi’s genius helped

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:51.900
shape the world rather than end it.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:56.720
He may have lived a relatively short life,
but Fermi did enough work in his five decades

00:24:56.720 --> 00:25:10.480
to fill hundreds of lifetimes.

